WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:02.490
Let’s get right to the point, shall we?

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:06.730
Is JavaScript’s garbage collector as bad
as some people think it is?

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:11.280
I have no idea, so let’s poke at this a
bit and see if we can learn something.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:17.640
Basically, when you allocate memory, in languages
like c++, you have to explicitly free it,

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.699
otherwise you have a memory leak.

00:00:19.699 --> 00:00:25.710
In languages like javascript, c#, java, go,
these are all garbage collected languages,

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:29.380
they handle a good chunk of the memory management
for you.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:33.950
When you allocate some memory, at some point
javascript has to decide you’re done with

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:34.950
it.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:39.560
So if I allocate an array, here’s my array
somewhere in memory, and then I fill that

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:44.300
array with stuff, so a number, a string, etc.
it really doesn’t matter.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:49.600
If I now null out one of the entries in the
array, this little blob of memory here kinda

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.739
gets left stranded now, it has no connection
to anything anymore, nothing refers to it

00:00:54.739 --> 00:01:00.309
In other words, it’s not reachable, if we
start at a root object, we can’t reach that

00:01:00.309 --> 00:01:05.790
little blob and thus it’s garbage, it’s
safe to reclaim that memory because nothing

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:08.110
can possibly be using it anymore.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:12.090
That’s the general idea anyway, let’s
see it in action with some code.

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:17.720
What I've done here is I've written a really
dumb test that just spam fills an object with

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.010
random strings and other objects.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:25.250
This is called via requestanimationframe,
kinda like you would in a game loop.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:30.490
So see here, I create data, set it empty to
start, then I loop over and create a bunch

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:34.509
of junk in there, basically a linked list
of objects with random names.

00:01:34.509 --> 00:01:36.840
And these all have to allocate memory.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:40.399
The strings take up memory, as do the objects
themselves.

00:01:40.399 --> 00:01:44.720
This “data” variable isn’t used anymore
at the end of each loop, so we’re achieving

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:48.399
our goal of wasting memory and forcing the
garbage collector to act.

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:53.680
So, in order to actually test this, you need
to be able to measure how much time javascript

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.939
spends in garbage collection.

00:01:55.939 --> 00:02:00.149
You could pull up dev tools and hit the performance
button and dig around there, but there’s

00:02:00.149 --> 00:02:03.149
actually another way that’s slightly less
known.

00:02:03.149 --> 00:02:08.660
This is chrome tracing, the backbone of chrome’s
performance testing framework, hundreds of

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:14.400
benchmarks measuring god knows how many thousands
of metrics are built on this.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:18.900
In another life, I was an owner here and even
led the infra team monitoring and processing

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.511
all this data.

00:02:20.511 --> 00:02:25.850
It’s pretty cool, you can see and attribute
time to various calls, see ipc’s between

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:29.769
processes, in general it just gives way more
info.

00:02:29.769 --> 00:02:36.349
For our purposes, I think it’ll just be
easier to focus on what we want to know, garbage

00:02:36.349 --> 00:02:38.040
collection events.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:43.819
So from here, you can start a trace, choose
which events you’re interested in, in our

00:02:43.819 --> 00:02:47.959
case, we only care about v8 stuff, and then
start recording.

00:02:47.959 --> 00:02:52.930
You just need to wait a bit to capture enough
of a trace, just capture however long you

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:55.750
want, don’t to wait to 100%.

00:02:55.750 --> 00:03:01.190
This uses the doom keys to move, WASD, so
let’s just zoom in and we can look a bit

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:04.459
closely at all these garbage collection events.

00:03:04.459 --> 00:03:09.180
And we can see we were successful at provoking
the garbage collector into action, all these

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.470
minorgc calls are v8 in action reclaiming
memory.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:16.640
If we’re lucky, maybe we see some majorgc
calls in here too.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:21.200
These are both versions of v8’s garbage
collector in action.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:27.230
So that brings up an important question before
we move on, what is a minor gc vs a major

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:28.230
gc.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:31.290
That’s a good question Simon, thanks for
asking that.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:36.630
V8 has a great blog post that talks in depth
about Orinoco, their garbage collector, and

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:39.800
goes through some of the high level details
on their collection strategy

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:45.819
There’s this idea, the generational hypothesis
in garbage collection, that most items die

00:03:45.819 --> 00:03:51.590
really young, also called infant mortality,
because why not just use super morbid terms

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:53.680
for memory management.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:59.510
But as a super handwavy explanation, v8 basically
divides allocations into new and old.

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:05.840
You have the new generation, and the old generation,
and in essence, allocations start in the new

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:06.879
generation.

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:12.799
So if I allocate a string here, it starts
out in new generation, specifically the nursery

00:04:12.799 --> 00:04:15.280
section part of this new generation.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:20.130
So then a garbage collection pass happens,
and let’s assume that some objects are reclaimed

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:21.380
and others aren’t.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:26.900
If that happens to survive the first garbage
collection attempt, it gets copied out, or

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.680
evacuated, into a sort of intermediate area.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:33.560
It’s not old yet, but it’s not brand new
either.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:37.700
Another garbage collection event occurs, bunch
of other stuff gets reclaimed.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:42.970
If at that point we find that our object survives,
if it’s still in use, it gets evacuated

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:44.890
out to the old generation.

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:50.930
V8 makes this distinction so that it can apply
separate strategies for the new and old generations.

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:57.820
A minorgc, the scavenger, is optimised to
collect from the new generation, while a majorgc,

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:02.060
which doesn’t have a cool name like the
mutilator or anything, does the heavy lifting

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:05.020
of sifting through the old generation.

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:07.970
Maybe you could call it the reaper or something.

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:12.431
So by looking at the trace of our first test,
we can actually select the first 10s or so

00:05:12.431 --> 00:05:19.450
of the trace, and we can see there were 384
occurrences of minorgc, average about 1.079ms

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:29.060
each, and 56 occurrences of majorgc, at around
0.838 ms each, so our test is getting v8 to

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:32.530
do both minor and major gc’s.

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:38.120
So we can change this test around just a tiny
bit to try to poke the majorgc into action.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:42.770
So what I’ve done here is I just take a
random subset of the data and I stuff it into

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:46.780
this.stuff, overwriting whatever happened
to be there.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:51.310
this.stuff is permanent, never deleted for
the duration of the test, so this should in

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:57.039
theory keep a bunch of random things for a
lot longer than a single call of DoSomethingStupid()

00:05:57.039 --> 00:05:59.900
and promote things to old generation

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:03.360
When we get a trace of the whole thing and
we’re just going to select the first 10s

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:08.849
or so of the trace, and we can see that this
is indeed what happens, we manage to force

00:06:08.849 --> 00:06:13.199
the garbage collector to work a bit harder,
and we’ve added some time to both minorgc

00:06:13.199 --> 00:06:15.020
and majorgc.

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:20.380
To try and understand the relationship between
allocation patterns and minorgc and majorgc,

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:22.360
I fiddled with this a bit more.

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:27.080
We’ll try creating fewer objects, but make
them more complex.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:32.319
This is the trace here, we’ll select the
first 10s again, and looking at it, minorgc

00:06:32.319 --> 00:06:37.860
isn’t changing a whole lot, but majorgc
is changing quite substantially, nearly doubling

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:40.620
the time taken vs the simpler cases.

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:45.650
I poke at this a bit more by adding a setup
step to the test, where I just allocate a

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:48.080
whole buncha random crap and store it.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:51.349
It’s never referenced in the test, but it’s
there.

00:06:51.349 --> 00:06:57.530
When we look at a trace of THAT, we see our
majorgc calls getting much more expensive.

00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:03.840
Although there’s fewer calls to majorgc,
each invocation is taking 26ms, which for

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:09.030
a game would be basically a skipped frame,
something that’d be inexcusable.

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:13.400
So that suggests there’s a relationship
between the just amount of crap you have allocated

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:19.060
and how long the gc takes, which I mean, is
hardly surprising, it has to figure out what

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:21.919
you’re using and not using, it’s not magic.

00:07:21.919 --> 00:07:28.259
If you were using another language where memory
management is explicit, such as c++, it wouldn’t

00:07:28.259 --> 00:07:34.139
matter how much or little you have allocated,
there’s no on-going cost for having it allocated,

00:07:34.139 --> 00:07:36.870
you only spend time allocating or freeing.

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:41.050
Whereas in JavaScript there’s definitely
like a tax on what’s currently allocated.

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:46.479
I tried a couple more experiments to see if
I could make minorgc’s more expensive, so

00:07:46.479 --> 00:07:50.430
what I did here was I allocated just random
stuff in a loop.

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:55.800
Looking at the trace, we DID spend quite a
bit of total time in minorgc’s, wall time

00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:01.080
is almost 10% of the time in fact, although
each singular call to minorgc was just shy

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:04.949
of 2ms, so not quite the crazy long time majorgc
took.

00:08:04.949 --> 00:08:11.280
I took another stab at it by allocating sparse
arrays, and just randomly setting values in

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:14.240
there, and v8 kinda laughed that attempt off.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:20.500
Nearly 800 minorgc calls, but they ran in
a pretty negligible amount of time.

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:26.650
I consider 10% to be pretty bad, but simultaneously,
it’s a ridiculous setup, so maybe instead

00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:31.490
let’s build a somewhat more plausible game
setup to see the effects of gc in action.

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:37.180
Here’s a quick and dirty game setup, so
what I've got here is a super basic bare bones

00:08:37.180 --> 00:08:39.150
“game” in quotation marks.

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:43.720
I don’t bother actually rendering any of
this, but we do simulate the entities.

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:49.019
So you’ve got this list of entities, we
call update on each one, when they die we

00:08:49.019 --> 00:08:51.770
destroy them, then filter them out.

00:08:51.770 --> 00:08:55.730
And we keep a stable population by creating
new ones in their stead.

00:08:55.730 --> 00:09:00.620
The entities themselves do some legit work,
so they have transform data associated with

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:06.150
each one, along with values like energy, health,
and they have a spatial hash grid client to

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:09.339
track their position in the world and query
other entities.

00:09:09.339 --> 00:09:13.970
They also have this update function, and then
I force them to do some random stuff.

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:19.270
I didn’t feel like making the logic extensive
so I just repeat it a few times.

00:09:19.270 --> 00:09:22.970
When they’re fully charged, they shoot off
a bullet, so they create a bullet, and that’s

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:24.200
added to the world.

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:28.150
And bullets are their own thing, they have
a full transform, their position is updated

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:32.579
according to velocity, and eventually they
explode and kill nearby things.

00:09:32.579 --> 00:09:33.700
So it does some stuff.

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:38.352
I took a few traces of this running, chose
a bad one, and we can poke around in here

00:09:38.352 --> 00:09:39.352
a bit.

00:09:39.352 --> 00:09:46.160
If I select the first 10s of the trace, we
can see some numbers for minorgc and majorgc.

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:52.519
We got around 500 or so minorgc calls, taking
on average less than half a ms, but adding

00:09:52.519 --> 00:09:55.269
up to around 240ms or so.

00:09:55.269 --> 00:10:01.160
There’s not many majorgc calls, barely any
actually, and the ones that do fire take a

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:04.269
little over 1.1 ms each.

00:10:04.269 --> 00:10:08.290
There was always going to be some overhead
for garbage collection, but at the same time,

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:13.620
this is written super poorly, realistically
an experienced game developer will code with

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:15.070
memory allocations in mind

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:17.710
So let’s make it a tiny bit better.

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:23.329
I’ve got a slightly improved version of
these entities that attempts to minimize allocations.

00:10:23.329 --> 00:10:28.519
In the main loop, we actually reuse entities
instead of allocating new ones, and the entities

00:10:28.519 --> 00:10:34.010
themselves use pre-allocated matrices and
vectors to perform their calculations.

00:10:34.010 --> 00:10:35.750
Nothing super crazy here.

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:40.500
Looking at a trace of that version though,
we can see a monster of a difference.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:46.700
Whereas before we had around 500 minorgc calls,
this version gets 110 or so, and a touch over

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:49.339
50ms total time spent there.

00:10:49.339 --> 00:10:54.410
There’s 1 majorgc call accounting for another
ms of time.

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:58.900
One really interesting thing that stands out
here is that, in the first example, let’s

00:10:58.900 --> 00:11:01.750
zoom in and look at these minorgc calls.

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:08.100
They’re happening DURING the frame, sometimes
multiple times, meaning they’re contributing

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:09.779
to longer frames.

00:11:09.779 --> 00:11:14.220
In the 2nd example, they’re happening in
the idle time between frames, which is totally

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:15.779
by design.

00:11:15.779 --> 00:11:21.110
V8 specifically built this feature into Chrome,
when it detects there’s an idle period,

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:25.380
that’s when v8 has the goahead to quickly
run gc.

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:30.130
What I kinda got from all of this was that
short lived objects are often pretty cheap

00:11:30.130 --> 00:11:31.589
from a gc perspective.

00:11:31.589 --> 00:11:36.161
That doesn’t mean they’re free though,
but if you can get them down low enough, they

00:11:36.161 --> 00:11:40.870
may just run in the idle time between frames
and you may not see much in terms of overhead

00:11:40.870 --> 00:11:42.209
for it.

00:11:42.209 --> 00:11:46.560
The more crap you keep in memory, the longer
lived cached stuff, the more expensive your

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:51.029
majorgcs are, as we saw with my stupid synthetic
test.

00:11:51.029 --> 00:11:56.790
I know my own code though, usually, so this
could probably be at least partially mitigated

00:11:56.790 --> 00:12:01.870
in the future by just letting me tell you
via some sort of api that gives hints to the

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:02.870
garbage collector.

00:12:02.870 --> 00:12:08.430
Something like that, I dunno, I’ve given
this a full 10 seconds of thought, you’d

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:11.220
need to actually plan this out more.

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:15.320
One major problem with javascript though is
that, although for your own app you might

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:20.529
be able to control your allocations, with
3rd party stuff, you have very little control,

00:12:20.529 --> 00:12:22.350
as far as I know.

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:27.130
I mean, in something like C++, its the sorta
same deal, but at least you can override memory

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:32.350
allocation at a global level, and depending
on your application, extend this to 3rd party

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:34.070
libraries as well.

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:40.000
In JS, no such luck, I can’t isolate a third
party library to its own memory pool, so that’s

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:42.760
just something you have to live with.

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:46.900
Another is that, yeah of course there’s
a cost to having gc, direct management will

00:12:46.900 --> 00:12:49.560
be more performant when done right

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:54.140
But in reality, decently made games don’t
just allocate and free on a whim either, most

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:56.060
do it in a very controlled way.

00:12:56.060 --> 00:13:01.010
I’ve spent enough time moving systems to
different allocation patterns, linear allocators,

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:07.260
pool allocators, LIFO and scratchpad style
frame allocators in order to wring out extra

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:08.260
performance.

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:13.440
C++ programmers may love to toot that horn,
but even among gamedevs, that was usually

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:17.500
handled by just a few people at the studio,
not everyone.

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:22.190
In JS, you have some control over memory,
but it’s pretty tenuous.

00:13:22.190 --> 00:13:24.839
Mostly it’s down to your pattern of allocation.

00:13:24.839 --> 00:13:29.220
Minimizing allocations by reusing objects,
that sort of thing.

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:34.270
Maybe you could go crazy using typed arrays,
but let’s save that for another time.

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:39.100
And lastly, how much memory overhead does
js incur for doing all of this?

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:40.870
How’s performance on FireFox?

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:43.000
etc. etc.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:44.000
Great questions!

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:45.389
No idea, didn’t test those.

00:13:45.389 --> 00:13:46.630
Maybe next time.

00:13:46.630 --> 00:13:50.209
Throw some comments about what you’d like
to see tested in the future.

00:13:50.209 --> 00:13:51.540
Until next time,
Cheers

